      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sanderson was founded in 1882 and originally called Strobridge before the railroad roundhouse prompted the name change to honor Thomas P. Sanderson.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanderson%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A 1906 courthouse designed by Henry Phelps still stands, later modified in 1932, 1950, and 1983, reflecting the town’s evolving architecture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanderson%2C_Texas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1931 Art Deco high school, facing the courthouse square, remains largely unchanged and showcases early 20th-century design.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanderson%2C_Texas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In June 1965 a flood on Sanderson Creek killed 26 people, after which several flood-control dams were built upstream to protect the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanderson%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Two tornadoes struck Sanderson on June 2, 2024, one EF1 and one EF3, damaging homes and injuring 12 people.",
